# Build-context excludes. Without this, `COPY . .` copies the
# developer's whole backend directory into the image.
#
# .env is the one that matters: docker-compose.yml *requires* a
# backend/.env (its `env_file:` entry), so on any machine following the
# documented local-dev path this file exists and holds real values —
# SECRET_KEY, the database password, and whatever SMTP credentials get
# configured once email delivery is decided. Copying it in would bake
# them into an image layer. Nothing in the image reads it anyway:
# config/settings.py reads os.environ only (no dotenv loader), and
# `env_file:` is applied by the Docker CLI at container-create time from
# the host, not from the image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

*.log
.DS_Store
